America and the world experienced a  double shock this week with the word  that two famous names had taken their  own lives on Tuesday fashion designer  Kate Spade was found dead in her  residence here in New York and just  yesterday the body of celebrity chef and  author Anthony Bourdain was discovered  in his hotel room in France the deaths  come as the Centers for Disease Control  published a study this week showing  suicide rates in the u.s. are on the  rise in almost every state here to  discuss the issue is psychiatrist dr.  Gail Saltz author of the power of  different the link between disorder and  genius scale good morning welcome good  morning so give us some idea are there  risk factors that we can look for  because we keep saying everybody looks  so happy on the outside right absolutely  most people will have been suffering for  some period of time with depression  anxiety bipolar disorder or they're  struggling with substance abuse they've  had a history of childhood trauma  they've thought about suicide in the  past they have a family member who  committed suicide or somebody in their  community has committed suicide so if  these things are going on you really  have to be willing to talk with the  person about what might be happening  watch for changes in their behavior or  anything well so if you see someone  becoming socially more isolated  withdrawing if you see if they're  talking about being in unbearable pain  if you if you see that they're suffering  in some way they're feeling sad they're  feeling hopeless they're feeling  worthless yes then those are people you  want to be talking to you absolutely so  how do you begin that very difficult  conversation with a loved one who may be  suffering from some mental illness I  think that you say quite frankly can I  talk to you I really notice that you're  suffering or you're down or I noticed  things and I want to listen I want to be  here please tell me what you're feeling  when they start to talk about it then  you say have you thought about harming  yourself have you thought about taking  your life being really direct it is a  myth that asking about suicide will  cause someone to commit suicide you want  to ask them and then frankly if they  affirm it if they say yes I've had those  thoughts then you ask them do they have  a plan do they have a means if they do  say they have a plan or it means you  want to take that away you can save a  life by frankly removing the gun  removing the pills taking away their  method in the moment because it can be  an impulsive act and then you want to  say hey I really want to take you to get  some help  help them to find the place they can go  the therapist they can see the emergency  room they can go to and take them if  need be  are people who if you have this  difficult conversation and your  experience is someone who is considering  taking their own life willing to talk  about it to someone who may approach  them and say what's going on with you  sometimes no but most often yes it's the  feeling that they won't be understood  that they won't be listened to that  they'll be ashamed that they'll be  stigmatized if you in a very open way  maybe even say like look I've I've had  difficult times too I really understand  but mostly are listening and empathic  believe me people are very willing to  talk about it if you open that door for  them better off to ask the question than  have a regret later  dr. Gail Saltz thank you very much if  you or someone you know is in distress  the national suicide prevention hotline  is there to help it is available 24  hours a day seven days a week and it is  confidential the number is on your  screen one eight hundred two seven three  eight two five five
 

